<!-- wp:column {"width":"66%","style":{"spacing":{"blockGap":"var:preset|spacing|4"}}} -->
<div class="wp-block-column" style="flex-basis:66%"><!-- wp:paragraph {"style":{"typography":{"lineHeight":"1.7"}},"textColor":"muted-foreground","fontSize":"base"} -->
<p class="has-muted-foreground-color has-text-color has-base-font-size" style="line-height:1.7">Nightjar sold forty-one coffees through three channels and had never agreed on how to name any of them. Wholesale buyers received a spec sheet, the shop wrote a chalkboard each morning, and the website invented its own vocabulary somewhere in between. Before drawing a single label we spent two weeks in the roastery building one naming system the whole company could live with.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph {"style":{"typography":{"lineHeight":"1.7"}},"textColor":"muted-foreground","fontSize":"base"} -->
<p class="has-muted-foreground-color has-text-color has-base-font-size" style="line-height:1.7">The identity that followed is deliberately plain: one monospaced typeface, a fixed grid of four label sizes and a printed swatch book so the production team can set a new release without calling us. Nine months on, Nightjar has shipped twenty-two coffees through the system and we have not opened a file since handover.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph --></div>
